(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-respiratory-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Respiratory Infections and Vaccines Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is clindamycin reserved as an alternative for lung abscess?","choices":["Its bronchospasm risk","Its C. difficile risk","Its poor anaerobic coverage","Its QT prolongation risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is kept for selected alternatives because of its C. difficile risk. The usual first choice is a beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor, such as intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam followed by oral amoxicillin-clavulanate. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 56, 22"},{"q":"Which treatment fits a high-risk outpatient with mild coronavirus disease 2019, without hypoxemia, on day 3 of symptoms?","choices":["Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir","Inpatient dexamethasone","Molnupiravir as first choice","Baricitinib with a steroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High-risk outpatients with mild or moderate disease and no hypoxemia take nirmatrelvir/ritonavir within 5 days, or a 3-day intravenous remdesivir course within 7 days of symptom onset, after review of drug interactions, renal dosing and hepatic contraindications. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 120, 122"},{"q":"In a child with influenza, which antipyretic is avoided because of its association with Reye syndrome?","choices":["Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Oseltamivir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is avoided in children younger than 18 because it is associated with Reye syndrome. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen is used as the antipyretic, and antibiotics are added only if bacterial pneumonia is proven or suspected. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What treats mild, isolated pulmonary cryptococcosis?","choices":["Itraconazole","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Amphotericin B plus flucytosine","Fluconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mild isolated pulmonary cryptococcosis is treated with fluconazole, generally for 6 to 12 months. Severe pulmonary, central nervous system or disseminated disease gets liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine induction, then fluconazole. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which test is used to diagnose histoplasmosis?","choices":["Routine bacterial culture","Urine Histoplasma antigen","Serum cryptococcal antigen","Serum beta-D-glucan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Urine Histoplasma antigen is used, often with serum antigen and antibody testing. Early negative tests do not exclude disease, and the antigen can cross-react with Blastomyces; culture or histopathology may confirm severe, persistent or uncertain cases. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"In pneumonia, what is the role of systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["Considered for severe cases, except influenza","Given routinely to every hospitalized patient","Required for influenza pneumonia","Reserved for nonsevere outpatient cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Routine corticosteroids are avoided in nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia, and systemic corticosteroids may be considered for severe community-acquired pneumonia, excluding influenza pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are Horder spots, seen in psittacosis?","choices":["Rose-spot-like facial macules","Vesicles on the trunk","Petechiae on the palate","Target lesions on the palms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Horder spots are macular rashes that resemble the rose spots of typhoid fever but appear on the face. Psittacosis can also bring relative bradycardia, splenomegaly and anemia from hemolysis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 83, 82"},{"q":"Although Pneumocystis is classified as a fungus, which class of drug is primarily used to treat Pneumocystis pneumonia?","choices":["Antivirals","Antibiotics","Azole antifungals","Amphotericin B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is classified as a fungal pneumonia, yet antibiotics are primarily recommended for mild, moderate or severe disease. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the preferred treatment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 58, 71"},{"q":"Which finding by itself marks community-acquired pneumonia as severe?","choices":["Age of 65 years or older","A positive viral test","Vasopressor-dependent shock","Two minor severity criteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe community-acquired pneumonia is signaled by vasopressor-dependent shock, by mechanical ventilation, or by at least three minor severe-pneumonia criteria. It needs urgent intensive-care-level assessment and a beta-lactam combination regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Before choosing a respiratory fluoroquinolone for pneumonia, which risk is reviewed along with allergy and interactions?","choices":["Recent influenza vaccine","QT prolongation","Egg allergy","Polyvalent cation intake"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin), allergy, QT, drug interactions and fluoroquinolone risks are reviewed first. The fluoroquinolone is one choice for outpatients with comorbidities. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the usual antibiotic duration for hospital-acquired or ventilator-associated pneumonia with an adequate response?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The usual duration for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia is 7 days, adjusted to clinical response. Cultures are obtained, therapy is reassessed daily, and it is narrowed or stopped as results clarify the diagnosis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 42, 46, 49"},{"q":"For suspected Pneumocystis pneumonia with a negative induced sputum and persistent high suspicion, which test comes next?","choices":["Lung biopsy","Pulmonary function testing","Bronchoalveolar lavage","Expectorated sputum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A negative induced specimen does not exclude Pneumocystis pneumonia; bronchoalveolar lavage is considered when induced sputum is negative or unavailable and suspicion remains high, sending microscopy or polymerase chain reaction testing. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 67, 69, 68"},{"q":"In the CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score, which blood urea nitrogen value earns a point?","choices":["Above 10 mg/dL","Above 20 mg/dL","Above 30 mg/dL","Above 40 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Urea above 20 mg/dL (7 mmol/L) earns 1 point; urea means blood urea nitrogen in mg/dL or serum urea in mmol/L. The other points are confusion, respiratory rate of 30 or more, low blood pressure and age 65 or older. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which test is timely and sensitive for psittacosis?","choices":["Routine bacterial culture","A single serology sample","Liver enzyme levels","Respiratory amplification test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory nucleic acid amplification or polymerase chain reaction testing is timely and sensitive, coordinated with public health laboratories. Bird or poultry exposure is asked about first. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the role of antibiotics in uncomplicated acute bronchitis?","choices":["Needed after 3 weeks of cough","Started empirically for fever","Avoided in routine cases","Started for colored sputum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Routine antibiotics are not prescribed for uncomplicated acute bronchitis, regardless of cough duration or sputum color, because they do not improve the viral cough illness. Their harms include adverse effects, C. difficile infection and resistance. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 18, 126"},{"q":"Which influenza antiviral is preferred for hospitalized patients and for pregnancy?","choices":["Oseltamivir","Baloxavir","Inhaled zanamivir","Amantadine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oseltamivir is preferred for hospitalized, severe or progressive, and high-risk patients, including pregnancy, started promptly even after 48 hours and adjusted for renal function. Hospitalized patients generally receive oral or enteric oseltamivir rather than the alternatives. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 108, 109"},{"q":"A rapid influenza antigen test is negative during high community activity. What does that result mean?","choices":["Antivirals should be withheld","Viral culture is needed first","Influenza is not excluded","Influenza is excluded"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid antigen tests have limited sensitivity, so a negative result does not exclude influenza, especially during high community activity. Confirm with molecular testing when the result affects management, and do not withhold indicated antivirals while waiting. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 105, 104"},{"q":"In which age group is the incidence of pneumonia highest?","choices":["Adults aged 30 to 49","Adults aged 50 to 64","Adults over 65","Adolescents"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonia incidence is highest over age 65, which is one reason vaccination matters in older adults. Age is also a risk factor at the other extreme: young children and older adults are at higher risk. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 24, 28"},{"q":"Which test is needed to diagnose uncomplicated acute bronchitis?","choices":["A complete blood count","A sputum culture","A chest radiograph","None; it is clinical"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is clinical, made after pneumonia, asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation, influenza, coronavirus disease 2019 and pertussis are considered. Routine blood counts, sputum culture and chest radiography are unnecessary in uncomplicated cases. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph pattern is seen in most patients with Pneumocystis pneumonia?","choices":["Cavity with an air-fluid level","Unilateral lower-lobe consolidation","Lobar consolidation, air bronchogram","Diffuse bilateral perihilar infiltrates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In most patients with Pneumocystis pneumonia, diffuse bilateral infiltrates extend from the perihilar region. On the pneumonia classification table, Pneumocystis is the usual cause of interstitial pneumonia, a bilateral, diffuse fine reticular opacity. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 70, 33"},{"q":"What does a history of egg allergy require before influenza vaccination?","choices":["Avoiding influenza vaccination","Routine vaccine safety measures","An egg-free vaccine product","Vaccination under specialist supervision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Egg allergy does not require an egg-free product or any extra precautions beyond routine vaccine safety measures. An age- and health-appropriate product is chosen as for anyone else. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In a patient with an acute cough, which feature should prompt testing for pertussis?","choices":["Post-tussive vomiting","Cough lasting 2 to 3 weeks","Green or yellow sputum","Transient wheeze"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pertussis is considered with a paroxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting, a whoop or a relevant exposure. There is no need to wait for 4 weeks of cough; suspected pertussis gets targeted testing and treatment to reduce transmission. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 17, 18"},{"q":"What risk of death does a CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) total of 2 carry?","choices":["Under 3%","About 9%","15 to 40%","About 27%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A total of 2 points is moderate severity, with a risk of death of about 9%, and the pathway sends these patients to the hospital. A total of 0 to 1 is low severity (under 3%), and 3 to 5 is high severity (15 to 40%). — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 31, 29"},{"q":"Which adults should receive a respiratory syncytial virus vaccine?","choices":["65 or older, or 19 to 64 at increased risk","50 or older, regardless of any risk","Every adult, repeated each year","75 or older, or 50 to 74 at increased risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One dose is recommended for all adults 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 at increased risk of severe respiratory syncytial virus disease. Repeat annual dosing is not currently recommended. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"Which symptom pattern is typical of Pneumocystis pneumonia?","choices":["Exertional dyspnea, dry cough","Foul-smelling purulent sputum","Sudden fever with a sore throat","Hemoptysis as the leading symptom"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pneumocystis pneumonia brings progressive exertional dyspnea (95%), a nonproductive cough (95%) and fever (over 80%), with chest discomfort, weight loss and chills; hemoptysis is rare. The symptoms are nonspecific. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the usual first choice of therapy for a lung abscess?","choices":["Chest physiotherapy as essential therapy","A beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor","Clindamycin as the routine first choice","Drainage or surgery as the first step"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor is the usual first choice, such as intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam followed by oral amoxicillin-clavulanate, for several weeks guided by clinical and radiographic response. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 56, 38"},{"q":"In coronavirus disease 2019, an oxygen saturation below 94% on room air with infiltrates over 50% of the lung defines which severity?","choices":["Mild","Moderate","Severe","Critical"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe illness is an oxygen saturation below 94% on room air, marked tachypnea or infiltrates over 50% of lung volume. Moderate illness has a positive radiograph with saturation of at least 94%; critical illness means respiratory failure, septic shock or multiple organ dysfunction. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which test confirms suspected pneumonia?","choices":["A complete blood count","A sputum culture","Molecular viral testing","Chest radiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suspected pneumonia is confirmed with chest radiography; lung ultrasound is an acceptable alternative where expertise exists. Imaging confirms a parenchymal process and separates pneumonia from uncomplicated bronchitis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 125"},{"q":"What is the core problem in aspiration pneumonitis?","choices":["Infection by oral flora","Anaerobic lung abscess","Viral airway inflammation","Sterile chemical injury"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspiration pneumonitis is sterile chemical injury, often after gastric contents enter the lungs, with abrupt respiratory decline after a witnessed aspiration. Fever or leukocytosis can occur without any bacterial infection. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 51, 50"},{"q":"Which measure helps prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia?","choices":["Frequent ventilator circuit changes","Delaying extubation","Keeping the patient flat","Elevating the head of the bed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention is the main thing for ventilator-associated pneumonia: early extubation, hand washing and sterile technique, limiting circuit changes, proper positioning with the head of the bed elevated, secretion drainage, particularly in the supraglottic area, and mouth care. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"}]};
